From uac421cf36d1a4c37 at groups.google.com  Tue Sep 13 12:02:03 2022
From: uac421cf36d1a4c37 at groups.google.com (Elliot Tucker-Drob)
Date: Tue, 13 Sep 2022 12:02:03 +0000
Subject: Re: Downward bias in covariance matrix using genomic controlled summary statistics
Message-ID: <y_UrPMKTY0U._lHyY1tQCgAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <y_UrPMKTY0U.__WC16FCCgAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
References: <y_UrPMKTY0U.eUUwOSMyCAAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com> <y_UrPMKTY0U.__WC16FCCgAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/genomic-sem-users/c/y_UrPMKTY0U/m/_lHyY1tQCgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The intercept indexes inflation of the chisq(1) statistic beyond its expected null of 1.0. Values above 1.0 represent inflation, i.e. more significant effects than expected. By multiplying the SE by the sqrt of the inflated intercept, the SE becomes larger, thereby making effects a bit less significant to counteract the inflation. The correction for the SE is sqrt of the intercept because the intercept indexes inflation of chisq, the sqrt of which is beta/SE (i.e. Z).

